  <Properties>
    <mainContent>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "7e5b24ac-fc8e-4b56-93b7-9820810ef7d7",
      "key": "32ea799d-0bf1-4a0a-aba1-9ce0e608aeea",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch2\u003EDay one: just the beans\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIt began innocently. Perry bought a small scale with a timer and a pad that does not melt, and set it beside the kettle. For the first week, every dose was 18 grams and every pour was 280. The coffee was better. The scale was smug about it.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBy the second week, Perry had started weighing the milk, then the sugar, then the cereal, which came out at precisely half the weight the box claimed. This was not a moral victory, but it was satisfying in a small, accountant-like way.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EWhere it went wrong\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIn week three, Perry guessed the weight of a banana before putting it on the scale. The guess was within four grams, and it felt like winning a prize that nobody else had entered. In week four, a friend handed over a gift and Perry weighed it on the way into the kitchen, just to see.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe upshot is that the scale has improved the coffee, and the rest of the household has complained steadily. Perry has agreed to stop weighing guests\u0027 luggage. The toast is still under review.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThere is a lesson in all this. Precision is addictive, and the only cure is a brew that tastes good enough to let the numbers go.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
